print("Welcome to practice questions chapter 2\n--------------\nBy Sophia Luo")
print("-------------\n1. What are the two values of the Boolean data type? How do you write them?\nThe two values are True and False. They are written with capital first letters: True and False.\n-------------")
print("-------------\n2. What are the three Boolean operators?\nThe three Boolean operators are: and, or, not.\n-------------")
print("-------------\n3. Write out the truth tables of each Boolean operator.\nand:\nTrue and True = True\nTrue and False = False\nFalse and True = False\nFalse and False = False\n\nor:\nTrue or True = True\nTrue or False = True\nFalse or True = True\nFalse or False = False\n\nnot:\nnot True = False\nnot False = True\n-------------")
print("-------------\n4. What do the following expressions evaluate to?\n(5 > 4) and (3 == 5) = False\nnot (5 > 4) = False\n(5 > 4) or (3 == 5) = True\nnot ((5 > 4) or (3 == 5)) = False\n(True and True) and (True == False) = False\n(not False) or (not True) = True\n-------------")
print("-------------\n5. What are the six comparison operators?\nThe six comparison operators are: ==, !=, <, >, <=, >=.\n-------------")
print("-------------\n6. What is the difference between the equal to operator and the assignment operator?\nThe equal to operator (==) compares two values, while the assignment operator (=) assigns a value to a variable.\n-------------")
print("-------------\n7. Explain what a condition is and where you would use one.\nA condition is an expression that evaluates to True or False. It is used in control flow statements like if, elif, and while to decide what code runs.\n-------------")
print("-------------\n8. Identify the three blocks in this code:\nBlock 1:\nif spam == 10:\n    print('eggs')\n    if spam > 5:\n        print('bacon')\n    else:\n        print('ham')\n    print('spam')\n\nBlock 2:\nif spam > 5:\n    print('bacon')\n\nBlock 3:\nelse:\n    print('ham')")
print("-------------\n9. Write code that prints Hello if 1 is stored in spam, prints Howdy if 2 is stored in spam, and prints Greetings! if anything else is stored in spam.\nspam = 1\nif spam == 1:\n    print('Hello')\nelif spam == 2:\n    print('Howdy')\nelse:\n    print('Greetings!')\n-------------")